# 5. CRITICAL PRIORITY — HAIRSTYLE SHAPE

The owner places high importance on hairstyle geometry.

Do not treat the hairstyle as a minor decorative feature.

The supplied Database character has a distinctive layered dark chestnut-brown hairstyle.

The hair is NOT a flat solid cube.

## 5.1 Main silhouette

Create:

- Large but controlled upper hair volume.
- Multiple stepped voxel hair layers.
- Slightly asymmetrical fringe.
- Irregular geometric hair contour.
- Softly tousled technical-professional hairstyle.
- Compact side layers.
- Clearly modeled back silhouette.

The hair should be volumetric from every direction.

Do not make it a helmet-shaped rectangular cap.

## 5.2 Front hair

From the front:

- Use several visibly layered fringe blocks.
- Slightly vary the projection of individual clusters.
- Frame the forehead naturally.
- Maintain sufficient space for the eyes.
- Preserve the recognizable dark brown fringe silhouette.

The fringe should feel intentionally styled, not randomly assembled.

## 5.3 Hair crown

The upper hair should contain:

- A main dark brown volume.
- Stepped layers of different heights.
- Small angular elevated clusters.
- Subtle asymmetry.
- A coherent overall shape.

Avoid perfectly identical rectangular blocks repeated across the crown.

Avoid excessive jagged spikes.

## 5.4 Left and right side

The side silhouette should have:

- Recognizable stepped layers.
- Compact volume near the temples.
- Correct relationship to the ears.
- Natural progression toward the back of the head.

Do not allow hair to cut through the ears.

## 5.5 Back silhouette

The rear hair MUST be deliberately modeled.

Use:

- Dark layered back volume.
- A thicker upper section.
- Lower stepped sections.
- Slightly tapered lower contour.
- Small geometry depth variations.

Do not leave a single featureless cube at the back.

The sheet contains a proper rear view; use it.

## 5.6 Hair color palette

Use a restrained dark-brown palette.

Suggested tonal family:

- Deep chocolate brown.
- Dark chestnut.
- Near-black brown shadows.
- Muted warm brown highlights.
- Very restrained burgundy-brown variation.

Avoid:

- Uniform pure black.
- Bright red highlights.
- Orange hair.
- Glossy wet-hair effects.
- Gray/silver hair associated with the CEO character.

The tone variation must complement the room lighting.

## 5.7 Hair material quality

Use mostly matte materials.

Recommended:

- High roughness.
- Low metallic response.
- Consistent shading.
- Carefully controlled material colors.
- Crisp voxel edges.

Do not simulate realistic individual hair strands.

Use actual geometric layering.

## 5.8 Hair geometry stability

Inspect:

- Hair-to-head intersections.
- Hair-to-ear intersections.
- Duplicate surface planes.
- Hair layer overlap.
- Hair shadow artifacts.
- Small decorative voxel clipping.
- Surface flickering during animation.

Avoid coplanar polygons.

Do not globally apply polygonOffset to solve hairstyle problems.

Correct faulty geometry directly.

## 5.9 Hair turnaround quality gate

Inspect the character from:

1. Front.
2. Front-right.
3. Right.
4. Back-right.
5. Back.
6. Back-left.
7. Left.
8. Front-left.

Confirm that the hairstyle remains recognizable in ALL views.

If only the front silhouette looks good, the hairstyle is not complete.

